00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.110
just cleaning out old pictures on my phone

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.840
and I found it where like the Khan Academy

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.470
was signed on a little grease board

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.103
like in a shared office space or something

00:02:53.103 --> 00:02:56.580
so it was like a funny, funny
that I found it yesterday

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.770
right before we got on this today

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.680
so it's really fun for me to be with you.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.440
The sports business,
as least as we operate,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.560
we're a service business
so we have two kinds of,

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.010
two groups of clients if you will

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.560
which is how most sports
marketing agencies operate.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.318
Either you can represent talent
which could be an athlete,

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:16.163
a coach, a broadcaster
in and around sports

00:03:16.163 --> 00:03:18.880
obviously if you were beyond
sports it would be a musician

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.810
or an actor or writer or director

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:24.010
but in our case we're just
in sports so it's athletes,

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.566
it's 2,500 athletes across 35 sports

00:03:26.566 --> 00:03:28.870
in our case which makes us
the biggest sports agency

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:29.703
in the world

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.200
and then the other side of the business

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.440
is how brands spend their money in sports

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.890
so very similar to how ad
agencies work for brands,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:39.723
we work for brands

00:03:39.723 --> 00:03:42.860
on how they spend their
sponsorship budgets, strategy,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.110
the execution of those
arrangements, amplification

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.310
and then ultimately measurement

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.500
and then the other side of it is

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.360
how do properties attract those dollars

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.120
so if you're a team, a league, a property

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.900
and you wanna understand
what brands are thinking,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.500
how they wanna spend their dollars,

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.410
what kind of assets they
need to tell their story

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.000
to connect with their consumers,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.700
we help them structure and package that

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.438
and so that's sports marketing

00:04:03.438 --> 00:04:05.830
kinda in its broadest sense of the word.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.127
We operate pretty much across
the entirety of the business.

00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:10.690
We have a thousand
employees around the world

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.220
and sports obviously is a,
while it's a global business,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.830
it truly manifests itself locally

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.040
so if you're gonna be
relevant and present in sports

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.800
or in regions you have
to be in those places

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.800
which is sort of given the
desperate nature of our business.

00:04:25.852 --> 00:04:28.930
- And what is the day-to-day
like of of what you do you know

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.130
someone who works at
Wasserman Agency, I mean,

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.780
you might have to do a lot of things

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.070
but you know for those of us
who are naive to this world

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.600
you know, we know the sports figures,

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.880
we know they play games,

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.570
we know that they, you know,
they get paid salaries,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.260
we know there's some sense of economics

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.960
that there's advertising,
there's sponsorship,

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.704
there's ticket sales so
what does an agency do

00:04:50.704 --> 00:04:54.110
for the various parties
especially for the sports figures

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:55.620
is at least how I conceptualize it?

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.440
- Yes, so for an athlete I mean,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.710
you might imagine different
than other kinds of talents

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.210
because team sports athletes
and individual sports athletes

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:05.320
by the way are a little bit different

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.629
so team sports athletes have
a natural support structure

00:05:07.629 --> 00:05:10.420
you know, you're in the
bay area, Clay Thompson

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.770
who's a client, The Warriors
are his support structure

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:13.603
during the season.

00:05:13.603 --> 00:05:16.456
There's doctors, there's
food, there's travel,

00:05:16.456 --> 00:05:18.900
there's obviously basketball,
there's health and wellness,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.003
there's a traveling support team

00:05:21.003 --> 00:05:23.609
when they're on the road all that stuff

00:05:23.609 --> 00:05:27.840
and so the agent is there to
deal with the off the court

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.640
opportunities that
happen during the season.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.820
Any other things that
might be an opportunity

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.240
as it relates to basketball,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.910
if there's you know obviously
he wouldn't be a player

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:38.220
who would get traded
but players get traded

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.887
if they get injured, things happen

00:05:39.887 --> 00:05:41.410
and then in the off season

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.000
we help them sort of manage their lives.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.130
Now on the individual athletes side

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.815
like a professional golfer,
we help coordinate travel,

00:05:47.815 --> 00:05:52.020
food, lodging, all that stuff setting,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.580
the competitive schedule
actually entering them

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.751
in the tournaments, hiring
and firing of caddy,

00:05:55.751 --> 00:05:59.773
dealing with sponsors,
arranging photo shoots,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.092
fixing problems when they happen,

00:06:03.092 --> 00:06:05.350
in many cases managing social media,

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.813
triaging social media problems
if they happen you know,

00:06:08.813 --> 00:06:10.870
they wanna invest in a business,

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:11.860
they wanna buy an apartment

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.530
or a house that's across all athletes,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.980
they wanna get married
or divorced or you know

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.930
there's a whole slew of things, you know,

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.030
I'm not sure the average age
of the people watching this

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.306
but if you've seen Jerry Maguire,

00:06:22.306 --> 00:06:26.410
it's a pretty good
representation maybe not in style

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.453
but in substance.

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.866
- No, I think that was
most of our first exposure

00:06:30.866 --> 00:06:34.450
and for me only exposure to this

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.701
but it is fascinating
'cause you imagine you know,

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:39.630
there's all these contract negotiations

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.170
and you read about it, you know,

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.370
actually I think would be great
math example word problems

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.300
of like if you win the
championship this happens

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.040
and if that happens, if you
get injured this happens.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.103
There are these you know,
that's what I imagine

00:06:50.103 --> 00:06:53.690
and you know where the
agents are very powerful

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.211
and important and most agents
have a background as lawyers,

00:06:57.211 --> 00:06:58.668
if I understand.

00:06:58.668 --> 00:07:00.260
- Yeah, yeah a lot are lawyers.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.366
I would tell you to your point
about a sort of math lesson

00:07:02.366 --> 00:07:06.570
you know, as you know
analytics and statistics

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.100
and data have become such
a big part of sports.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.330
Obviously Moneyball started it in baseball

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.601
but it's now pervasive in sport.

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:18.060
That's how teams negotiate,
that's how they perceive value

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.610
and so we view as an
agency our responsibility

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.170
is to act on behalf of
our clients as you know

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.360
take the NFL as the 33rd team, right

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:29.100
so we have to have the
resources and data and analytics

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.160
and statistics and analysis
and financial understanding

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.070
of television contracts and
sponsor deals and local revenue

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.362
and national revenue and how
those all feed into each other,

00:07:37.362 --> 00:07:40.410
salary cap projections, how
long of a deal should you make,

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.240
all those things so that we
can serve our clients best

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.811
because it has really become
a very much data, statistics,

00:07:47.811 --> 00:07:52.120
financial driven model
and we need to do our best

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.180
to represent our clients
to achieve the best results

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.100
for them and it's really changed a lot.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:00.364
It used to be relationships
and you owe me one here

00:08:00.364 --> 00:08:02.710
because you signed that
guy and you underpaid him

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.110
so I needed to take care of my guy here

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.630
and those days are not over
but they're pretty much over

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.790
and so the whole,

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.080
if you will math of it
has changed dramatically

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.148
and ironically while a lot
of the agents are lawyers,

00:08:13.148 --> 00:08:14.914
the legal part has been reduced

00:08:14.914 --> 00:08:17.333
in many ways the contracts for team sports

00:08:17.333 --> 00:08:20.637
are literally fill in the blank contracts,

00:08:20.637 --> 00:08:23.330
the addendums are where
all the math happens

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.240
and that's the interesting part

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.972
but that's as much analytics, it is legal

00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:29.303
so it's an interesting evolution
of the business for sure.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.340
- Now it is interesting 'cause
when you realize in sports

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.520
more than anything it's very measurable.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.020
The outcomes are very measurable,

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.500
the economics are very measurable

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.707
and so yeah it's probably
more than any other industry

00:08:39.707 --> 00:08:42.920
is suited for this type of analysis.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.482
There's a question coming off of YouTube

00:08:44.482 --> 00:08:46.797
from Sudha Rani says,

00:08:46.797 --> 00:08:49.040
"Hi, Casey when did you start?"

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.170
And so you know just how
did you get into this?

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.499
Did you always know that you
wanted to be a sports agent?

00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:56.339
What drew you into this profession?

00:08:56.339 --> 00:08:58.390
- I would say I always loved sports

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.740
like a lot of kids growing up

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:00.810
but I was a weird kid

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.178
and that I loved the business of sports,

00:09:02.178 --> 00:09:04.340
kind of an odd thing to love.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.440
I was, spent a summer as a bald
boy for the Cleveland Browns

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.851
and spent more time in the front office

00:09:09.851 --> 00:09:13.396
than I did on the field frankly

00:09:13.396 --> 00:09:17.480
so I've always loved the
business of sports and really...

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.139
In 2002, acquired our first little agency,

00:09:21.139 --> 00:09:22.915
a tiny little action sports agency

00:09:22.915 --> 00:09:25.150
that we still own today
that's quite big now

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:27.200
than the biggest action sports agency

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:28.100
and just built from there.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.040
We were opportunistic, we were strategic,

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:31.050
we made lots of mistakes

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:36.050
and built in a way that we
thought was gonna create value

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.550
for the client and make a
point of differentiation for us

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:40.610
in a competitive industry

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.005
and so started with a larks sports agency,

00:09:43.005 --> 00:09:46.440
bought a fairly big
basketball baseball agency

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.850
and then have bought a
ton of other agencies,

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.330
has hired lots of agents from competitors

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.030
and built a really
incredible group of business

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.840
based on great work of
about 130 sports agents

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:59.710
around the world and I'm
lucky enough to be able

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.860
to pursue and work in
my passion every day.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.575
- And so it sounds like you...

00:10:04.575 --> 00:10:07.223
It is what you imagined it to be?

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.870
- Yeah, I mean I would say
there are certainly things

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.720
that I do that I never imagined I would do

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.900
and certain things I get
to do that I never imagined

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:18.880
would be possible but what
I love about our business,

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:19.950
the diversity of our business,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:21.400
is that no day is the same Sal.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.746
We, I get to spend my days...

00:10:23.746 --> 00:10:26.306
I really think focus on three things.

00:10:26.306 --> 00:10:29.317
I focus on the strategy of the company,

00:10:29.317 --> 00:10:32.970
I focus on the culture which
is about the work environment

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.977
for the people and I deal with problems

00:10:34.977 --> 00:10:37.650
and if you think about human nature,

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.620
human nature is that you
stick your head in the sand

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:40.500
and hope problems go away

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:44.360
and the truth is problems
usually get worse

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.110
unless you deal with them
and so dealing with problems

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.114
but creating an organization
that actually supports people

00:10:49.114 --> 00:10:51.610
communicating about
problems and rewards people

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:53.310
as opposed to penalizing them,

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:54.560
kinda turns the paradigm on its head

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.730
and I think the more you do,
the more successful you are,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.670
the more problems you have
and the better you are

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.810
dealing with them is the
more successful you are

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:04.400
and so my days are, depends on
the day, depends on the week.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:05.880
You can imagine during this period of time

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:10.750
we've had obviously
unprecedented issues to deal with

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.439
on behalf of our clients,
on behalf of our business

00:11:12.439 --> 00:11:17.020
but from things like when
baseball players are trying to

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.900
come back to helping them think
about the economics of that,

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.390
the testing protocols you know,
using all of our resources

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:25.240
to help them make informed
decisions about playing.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.300
We have players who are
player reps for their teams

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:28.990
in terms of all, they
represent all the players

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:31.690
on their team so you know
the diversity of what I do

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.550
is what I love and I get
to do it in a business

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:35.450
I'm passionate about in a world of sports

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:37.840
that I think is you know in
a world where you get to,

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.010
you don't always get to pick what you do,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:41.763
I'm lucky to get to do it in
the world of sports every day.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:43.710
- And I wanna dig deeper into that,

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.030
I definitely wanna go
get into what's going on

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:48.040
because of COVID because
that's obviously fascinating

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.194
but there's you know a couple
of folks off of Facebook

00:11:50.194 --> 00:11:51.700
and YouTube, you know,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.850
you quickly alluded to some
mistakes and struggles you made

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.990
but you kinda gave the very quick you know

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:58.830
you read in a magazine you
know, Casey started here,

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.740
he graduated there and then he
became the head of an agency

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.600
and they're very successful
and you know happy every,

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.586
happily ever after but you know,

00:12:04.586 --> 00:12:05.920
it's never that way

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.817
and so you know from
Facebook Shaz Warwick asks,

00:12:09.817 --> 00:12:12.300
"What type of mistakes have you made?"

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:14.348
From YouTube Parole Mytha asking,

00:12:14.348 --> 00:12:17.347
"What are the struggles you
encountered on your way?"

00:12:17.347 --> 00:12:19.932
From YouTube Susanna Dominguez,

00:12:19.932 --> 00:12:22.037
"Could you describe a problem

00:12:22.037 --> 00:12:23.710
"and how you overcame that problem?"

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.360
So yeah, give us a little bit of texture,

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:26.193
fill in the blanks.

00:12:26.193 --> 00:12:28.766
Was this smooth sailing the whole time?

00:12:28.766 --> 00:12:31.370
- Never and it's, there's no such thing

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:32.620
and I think one of the
misconceptions people have

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.962
in this day and age of
perceived instant success

00:12:35.962 --> 00:12:38.790
is that success is a moment
in time and the truth

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.270
is my view of success is that

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.243
it comes from relentless consistency

00:12:43.243 --> 00:12:46.040
and that pursuit of excellence
over a long period of time

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.759
relentlessly and when I talk
about dealing with bad news,

00:12:48.759 --> 00:12:52.650
if you're able to
relentlessly pursue excellence

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.210
on a consistent basis it means
that the problems you have

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:56.160
you've been able to get through

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.027
and overcome and get to the other side

00:12:58.027 --> 00:13:00.380
and so we have, there is
you know, as I like to say,

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.470
no one has ever come into my office

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:03.740
to give me something for nothing,

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:07.940
that just doesn't happen and
the truth is we're a better

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:10.250
organization because we're
better at dealing with problems,

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.560
it's why we're able to get
through a time like COVID,

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:17.153
2009 you know I mean I've now been the...

00:13:17.153 --> 00:13:18.270
Started this business in 2002

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.021
so I've been running this
company for 18 years,

00:13:20.021 --> 00:13:23.444
this is now the second time in my career

00:13:23.444 --> 00:13:25.550
that I'm dealing with
something that was otherwise

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:27.530
supposed to be unprecedented,

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.240
that's unto itself unprecedented
and learning how to manage

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.730
through these things and ups
and downs and I can tell you

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:36.513
I was at the NBA all-star
game in February.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.580
I didn't think that
less than 30 days later

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.740
the NBA would shut down for
good until hopefully a week

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.643
from now and my business

00:13:45.643 --> 00:13:47.820
would be dramatically off in revenue,

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:50.520
I'd have to make all sorts
of difficult decisions

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.070
and our business by the way is I think

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:53.760
as stable and good and positive.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.390
We didn't lay off significant
numbers of people,

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:57.862
we had like less than two percent

00:13:57.862 --> 00:14:01.300
and that's really for
businesses that evaporated

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.617
because of this not
businesses that are just down

00:14:03.617 --> 00:14:06.390
and working through an environment

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:08.230
like this is really difficult

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.504
but by the way problems
don't have to be big,

00:14:10.504 --> 00:14:13.460
it can be a mistake by an
employee that you lose a client,

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:17.500
it can be a mistake by an
employee that causes a problem

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:21.311
internally in terms of culture
and the work environment.

00:14:21.311 --> 00:14:25.550
There are, it can be a mistake
someone posts something on

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:28.320
social media they shouldn't
post who's an employee you know,

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.240
in the end my name's on
the door and my reputation

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.740
is at stake and so all
those things are important

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.874
and we take responsibility
for them and we take

00:14:36.874 --> 00:14:40.930
focus on them in terms of
making sure we treat them

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:41.950
as we would all want to be treated

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:45.060
and there are literally problems every day

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.040
and so the reason I
think we have had success

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:49.670
is because we've been
doing it a long time,

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:50.970
I think we do it very well,

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.170
in the end we do it for
the benefit of our clients,

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:55.040
that's the only metric
we measure ourselves

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:56.370
by is the quality of work

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:58.500
and the longer you can do it,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:14:59.816
the more consistently you can do it,

00:14:59.816 --> 00:15:02.570
you can wake up today and
I have a thousand employees

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.424
and hundreds of millions
of dollars of revenue,

00:15:04.424 --> 00:15:07.460
the action sports
business we bought in 2002

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.380
had less than a million dollars of revenue

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.720
and now if you told me in 2002

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:11.860
we'd be doing what we're doing today

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:13.580
with the number employees
we were doing it,

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:14.620
I would have said, no way.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.167
I couldn't imagine how I'd get there

00:15:16.167 --> 00:15:18.390
and I hope in five years
I'll be able to look back

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:19.930
and say that's all we were doing

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:22.040
and now we're doing all this
and you I can't tell you how

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.450
we're gonna get to that
point but it's again,

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:27.380
it's one of the things
I see most amongst the,

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.400
a younger generation
I think you and I are,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.460
is they think that moment
of success is a moment

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.656
because of a singular action
and the truth is it never is.

00:15:35.656 --> 00:15:38.810
It's the result of a lot of
work over a long period of time

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:40.260
through lots of up and downs.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.746
- Did you ever have a moment
where you're just like

00:15:43.746 --> 00:15:45.910
I don't think I'm gonna
make it, this isn't for me,

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.263
I don't have what it takes?

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:51.330
- No but that's probably just you know

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:52.920
my blind competitiveness.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:56.070
I mean I am truly, crazy competitive

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.400
and so even if I was at the
brink of that happening,

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.670
I would never think that
'cause it's just not in my DNA

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.500
just like by the way enjoying success

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.210
is not particularly in my DNA either

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.800
so I would say I'm much more focused

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.288
on always driving forward.

00:16:10.288 --> 00:16:12.630
I spend very little time worried about

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.240
what's in the rear view
mirror, good or bad

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:17.660
and so it's not any anything
that makes me better

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:18.493
than anybody else,

00:16:18.493 --> 00:16:20.143
it's just in kind of in my DNA.

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.750
- I think we have some similarities.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:25.970
I have had my moments
but then I'd go on a hike

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:27.530
and I said that squirrel doesn't care

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.890
that I just failed miserably
and embarrassed you know

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:32.230
myself and everything
and then I get over it

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:34.500
and I get back on track.

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.980
Before we go to COVID

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:38.980
'cause I think there's a whole many things

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:40.470
we can talk about there you know,

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.230
what's I think interesting
about being a sports agent

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:44.770
is obviously you are running a business,

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:47.010
you are a manager, you're
a leader for your team

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.640
but you're also I could
imagine an observer

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.190
of what these elite athletes are like

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:54.170
and I'm curious you know

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:56.060
from as an outsider's point of view,

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.964
we say oh look you know
so-and-so has god-given talent

00:16:58.964 --> 00:17:01.080
so incredible et cetera, et cetera

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.332
but you see what goes
on behind the scenes,

00:17:03.332 --> 00:17:07.340
you know what's your observation
of what makes talent great?

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:08.720
What makes people have enduring

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.655
and really successful careers in sports

00:17:10.655 --> 00:17:12.360
and maybe what's surprising relative

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.660
to what most people think?

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.100
- Well I would say the
two things that are maybe

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:18.260
one surprising one not

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.670
but that one they are the elite athletes

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:25.670
are willing to make sacrifices
in some or significant parts

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:29.790
of their lives to be
the best at what they do

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:31.698
and people don't like to
admit that or own that

00:17:31.698 --> 00:17:34.150
but they're, you cannot do it all

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:36.630
and they are they have chosen
to make those sacrifices

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:40.150
to dedicate themselves to their
craft to be at that level.

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:44.250
Obviously if anybody watched
the last dance on ESPN

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:46.542
about Michael Jordan, he
made incredible sacrifices

00:17:46.542 --> 00:17:49.570
and incredible dedication to his craft

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:50.560
and that's what made him elite

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:52.470
in addition to clearly god-given talent

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.600
at a level maybe we've never seen before.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.237
The other piece of it is
athletes are human beings,

00:17:57.237 --> 00:18:00.670
they have all the same
insecurities, anxieties, fears,

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:05.180
concerns, worries, ups, downs,
issues with kids, partners,

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:08.730
loved ones, parents, finances, good, bad

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.248
like they are people and
all that stuff affects them

00:18:11.248 --> 00:18:13.923
and maybe they're better
at compartmentalizing them

00:18:13.923 --> 00:18:16.640
so that they can compete
but there are people

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:17.970
and they have all those issues too

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:20.290
so you know yes they can be role models,

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.447
yes they ought to carry
themselves in a way

00:18:22.447 --> 00:18:23.991
'cause they do have influence

00:18:23.991 --> 00:18:25.650
but we also need to understand.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:27.770
I think part of this you started
to see with the recognition

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:30.100
that mental health is something
that affects everybody,

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:32.120
not some people, is that athletes

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.500
are starting to communicate
about mental health concerns

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:35.920
and issues that they have anxiety.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:37.510
Kevin Love couldn't go on the court

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:39.070
because of an anxiety attack you know.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:40.490
Most people wouldn't have
been comfortable to talk

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:42.690
about that and yet tens
of millions of people

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.256
in this country probably
had that happen this morning

00:18:44.256 --> 00:18:48.620
and so they are people and I
think that surprises people

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:49.930
that they have all the same issues,

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.184
all the same stuff that
we all do every day

00:18:52.184 --> 00:18:55.140
and they're able to
function and do their jobs

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:56.870
and maybe that's what makes them talented

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:59.950
on their field of play
but they're people too

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:01.940
and they can have really
bad days just like

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:03.940
we all can and really good
days just like we all can

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:06.630
and it's important to
keep that perspective.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.720
- Yeah, that's super,
super valuable insight

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:12.890
and going into this COVID I
mean you alluded to it you know,

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:14.660
most professional sports have essentially

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:18.269
had to stop for the last
four, five, six months.

00:19:18.269 --> 00:19:20.552
I'm curious your view,

00:19:20.552 --> 00:19:23.000
well one how do you think
this is going to evolve?

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:27.261
I know that the NBA is doing
this kind of Disney world

00:19:27.261 --> 00:19:30.032
I mean, what, how do
you think it's gonna...

00:19:30.032 --> 00:19:31.440
How do you think it's gonna evolve

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:34.876
and let's just assume a
scenario where COVID lasts

00:19:34.876 --> 00:19:38.173
well into next year
potentially even longer?

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:43.000
- Yeah so a couple things.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:47.330
A bubble works, that's the good news

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:48.810
but it also is the bad news

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.420
because if you burst, if you
break the bubble you know,

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.640
you can get a really bad
situation really quickly.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.710
Two, we are woefully
under-prepared as a country

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:00.330
to test at the level we
test at with the consistency

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.550
and the speed and the
accuracy we need to test at

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.170
to absent a medical solution

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.090
returned to as much as normal as possible

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:09.990
so I compare baseball now playing 60 games

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.700
and empty stadiums, the NFL
likely to play its entire season

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:16.010
mostly in empty stadiums,

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.090
college football being completely canceled

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.330
probably if not delayed until the spring

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:23.905
and the premier league in England

00:20:23.905 --> 00:20:25.440
has announced they're gonna
play up to 50 capacity

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.136
and I think they might
even get close to 100%

00:20:27.136 --> 00:20:31.030
because the testing in the UK
has gotten so good, so quick,

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.540
so consistent, so accurate

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.150
and essentially everybody has an app

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:36.610
on their phone with their test results

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:38.010
so if you pass the test,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.903
you can go to the premier
league game that day

00:20:39.903 --> 00:20:42.777
and so testing is the
answer in the short term.

00:20:42.777 --> 00:20:44.666
Everybody you know,

00:20:44.666 --> 00:20:47.116
Nirvana would be everybody
tested every day.

00:20:47.116 --> 00:20:50.350
That solves 95% of the
problems we're in right now

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:52.860
in terms of people having a normal life

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.420
whether it's going to a sporting event

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.640
or going to a gym or going to
get you know get a manicure,

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.010
it doesn't really matter if we are testing

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:03.240
and we know and then being
responsible in your behavior.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.645
Masks, hand washing, all
those things it sounds trite,

00:21:06.645 --> 00:21:10.660
it's not political, it's fact, it works.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:13.590
Keep your distance, wear
a mask, wash your hands,

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:16.180
you're likely not to get
COVID even in an environment

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:18.590
where it exists and with pervasive testing

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:20.380
we can return to as much
as normalcy as possible

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:22.040
until there's a medical solution

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:24.300
whether that's in six months or two years

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.992
as you said and no one knows to be fair

00:21:25.992 --> 00:21:29.718
and so sports is at effect if you will

00:21:29.718 --> 00:21:31.580
until that testing
exists, we can't do that.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.330
You know there's enough testing to test

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:34.610
a thousand NBA players every day

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:36.620
that's fine or baseball players

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:37.740
but there's not enough tests

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.920
to test 300 million Americans every day

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:42.210
or every other day and
that's the problem we're in

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:45.600
and that's really one of
the great disappointments

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.760
I have about that here
we are five months later

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:48.680
and you know,

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:50.680
on the other hand we're
talking about vaccines

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:51.726
may be coming quickly

00:21:51.726 --> 00:21:54.438
but we're still only testing
three million people a day

00:21:54.438 --> 00:21:57.370
and I'm no math expert, I think you are

00:21:57.370 --> 00:21:59.720
but if you have to give the vaccine twice

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:00.650
like most vaccines do

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:02.770
and certainly like the
Oxford one appears to be,

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:05.123
that's 700 million
injections plus or minus.

00:22:05.123 --> 00:22:07.238
If you're only giving 3
million a day that's 200 days

00:22:07.238 --> 00:22:09.680
before you even get everybody vaccinated,

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:11.440
if everyone agrees, if you started today

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:13.273
and if you had all the
vials and all that you know

00:22:13.273 --> 00:22:14.740
like it's a real process here

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.880
so like I don't think we're prepared

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:20.020
and you know I went to
UCLA as John Wooden said,

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:22.123
failing to prepare is preparing to fail.

00:22:24.832 --> 00:22:27.580
- It's a, that's a good quote.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:29.850
What do you think is going
to happen to the sports?

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:31.030
I mean it sounds like the sports industry

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:33.153
is figuring out a way to proceed

00:22:33.153 --> 00:22:35.990
and I'm not an expert
in the economics of it,

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:38.100
is the bulk of the economics,

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:42.070
our TV and sponsorship so even
with empty stadiums it'll,

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:44.110
it won't have the same
energy or the same feel

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:46.550
but do you think it'll be okay?

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:49.360
- It will be okay, it
won't be obviously ideal

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:51.580
or great and it depends on the sports.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.260
Now some sports predominantly
get their revenues

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:55.950
from tickets and some sports predominantly

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:57.880
get their revenue from
TV and you can imagine

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:59.777
the further you are on the
TV end of the spectrum,

00:22:59.777 --> 00:23:01.387
the more stable you are

00:23:01.387 --> 00:23:04.030
and the further you are on the
ticket end of the spectrum,

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:05.410
the more you're gonna have problems

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:09.650
so a sport like tennis well
in its competitive sense

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.060
feels very safe 'cause
they are clearly far apart

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:13.800
from each other physically,

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.030
90% of the revenue comes from ticket sales

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:17.510
so that's why you haven't
seen a tennis match

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.370
and by the way players
from all over the world

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:21.900
traveling all over the
world that's a problem

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:24.470
and then a sport like the
NFL that has so much revenue

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:25.960
even though it's a majority

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:27.738
it's just a lot of ticket revenue,

00:23:27.738 --> 00:23:30.880
from television they can
play games and it's worth it

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.345
to play games and everything
else is kind of in the middle

00:23:34.345 --> 00:23:36.598
and the other piece of it is

00:23:36.598 --> 00:23:41.598
you can get through a year
or two of this at most.

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:43.970
I would tell you most
leagues are thinking about

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:47.710
for the 2021 season for
like the NBA and the NHL

00:23:47.710 --> 00:23:51.190
who start at the end of
'20 for their 2021 season

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:55.220
or the NFL and major baseball
will play complete seasons

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:56.490
in '21 or major league soccer,

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.290
I think they're figuring
maybe on a blended basis

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:01.970
you can have 30-40% fans
for the whole of a season

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:03.100
which may means you start out

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:04.712
at the beginning of the
season with no fans,

00:24:04.712 --> 00:24:06.840
get to a reduced capacity scenario

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:08.990
and then maybe by the end of the '21,

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:12.648
2021 seasons you can get pretty
close to full capacity so

00:24:12.648 --> 00:24:16.390
and then the next year after
get back to a sense of normalcy

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:19.070
in terms of capacity and filling stadiums

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:21.346
and that's obviously what
people are hoping for

00:24:21.346 --> 00:24:23.870
but hope doesn't solve
the problems which is why

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:24.810
we need to focus on things

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:28.083
like testing and responsible behavior.

00:24:29.331 --> 00:24:30.527
- That makes a lot of sense

00:24:30.527 --> 00:24:32.687
and you know, even before a COVID,

00:24:32.687 --> 00:24:34.673
what did you think was going to be

00:24:34.673 --> 00:24:38.210
kind of the evolution
or the future of sports?

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:40.250
You know, there's questions
here about things like VR

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:42.850
for sports viewing from Rickmon Facebook.

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:45.610
I mean is there a view
that this is how sports

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:46.603
are gonna look in five or 10 years

00:24:46.603 --> 00:24:49.513
and has COVID changed that view at all?

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:51.453
- Couple things.

00:24:51.453 --> 00:24:52.790
I think one of the things that hasn't.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:55.050
So a few, few things to
remember about sports,

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:57.180
I think it's truly the only defensible

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:59.020
piece of the media
business, it matters live,

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:02.268
it can't and won't be time
shifted, it's evergreen

00:25:02.268 --> 00:25:04.980
and all those things
that make it relevant,

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:06.120
it's truly local, right?

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.210
You love your local team it matters,

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.189
it's kinda embedded the
community in a special way

00:25:10.189 --> 00:25:12.770
and sports has really
been for the last 25 years

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:14.220
optimized for television

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:17.420
because the great majority
if not almost 100% of people

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:18.840
only consume sports on TV.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:22.860
I mean I think the NFL has a
stat that 97% of avid NFL fans

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:24.499
will never see a game in person

00:25:24.499 --> 00:25:27.563
and so you've seen the
television experience optimized,

00:25:27.563 --> 00:25:31.060
the in stadium experience relatively,

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:33.060
I mean if you wanna
look 20 years ago today

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:34.600
maybe the stadiums are newer and nicer

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:36.080
and the tickets are clearly more expensive

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:37.910
but the experience hasn't changed a lot.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:39.800
I think COVID will change
the experience a lot,

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:42.250
focus a lot more on
the customer experience

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:43.500
and I'm not just talking about health

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.440
and safety protocols which
clearly need to be better,

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:47.380
I'm talking about why
are we waiting in line

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.620
for 20 minutes for concessions?

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:50.075
Why do we wait lines for bathrooms?

00:25:50.075 --> 00:25:53.330
Why do we have cash and slow things down?

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:56.880
Why you know again what why
isn't the experience better

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:58.500
so that the customer gets more benefit?

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.600
All those things obviously
have health and safety benefits

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.910
but they have customer benefits

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:03.460
so I think that will happen for sure

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:05.550
and then the other thing
you're gonna you have seen

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:07.910
and we'll see I think
accelerate aggressively

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:10.580
is this sort of transition

00:26:10.580 --> 00:26:13.840
to different monetizations of content.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:17.470
So obviously we can watch
on TV free for the most part

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:18.889
or on some cable channels,

00:26:18.889 --> 00:26:21.210
there's gonna be direct to
consumer for all the leagues,

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:22.460
there's gonna be a lot more gambling,

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:25.160
there's gonna be a lot more
buying of short forms of games,

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:26.390
there's gonna be a lot more buying

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:28.645
of a couple minutes of the ends of games.

00:26:28.645 --> 00:26:31.190
I think the consumption of sports

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:33.840
is gonna continue to
accelerate and be pervasive.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.490
I think the points of
where you will consume it

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:39.160
will start to evolve slower
probably than people think

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:41.310
and I think ancillary ways to consume it

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:43.573
will probably accelerate
faster than people think.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:46.510
- Super, super interesting

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:48.742
and these conversations always
go much faster than I expect.

00:26:48.742 --> 00:26:52.150
I do wanna make sure
we get to the Olympics

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:53.375
'cause that's fascinating,

00:26:53.375 --> 00:26:55.770
I mean you're the chairman of the Olympics

00:26:55.770 --> 00:26:57.994
in Los Angeles in 2028.

00:26:57.994 --> 00:27:01.320
I guess a bunch of questions
I'll bundle together

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:02.879
you answered however you like,

00:27:02.879 --> 00:27:05.890
how did you get into that role
why were you interested in it

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:09.130
and kind of where do you see
is the value of the Olympics?

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:12.210
Like what, you know why
are you putting the time

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:14.200
and energy into it the way you are?

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:16.640
- So I was asked by Mayor
Garcetti of Los Angeles

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:20.280
to lead the bid which
was the summer of 2014

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:22.210
we started this process.

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.350
It's actually something I
thought was a, I joke with him,

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:27.004
that was a terrible idea
and that's why he's mayor

00:27:27.004 --> 00:27:28.414
and I'm not but that was a terrible idea

00:27:28.414 --> 00:27:29.929
and I thought we would lose
and it was a fool's errand

00:27:29.929 --> 00:27:32.090
and he convinced me that he's the mayor

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:32.970
and I don't really have a choice

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:35.051
so if I wanted my trash picked up,

00:27:35.051 --> 00:27:38.426
I better chair the bid so here I am.

00:27:38.426 --> 00:27:40.670
I happened to be a kid of Los Angeles.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:43.440
I was 10 years old in 1984 when
the Olympics were in my city

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:45.860
and I saw what an
incredible experience it was

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:47.430
so I absolutely loved the Olympics,

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:49.434
bidding for the Olympics is
a different kind of thing

00:27:49.434 --> 00:27:51.531
and now that we have the Olympics...

00:27:51.531 --> 00:27:55.670
Look being chair in my role in Los Angeles

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:56.960
is different than any other city on earth.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:00.750
We have the physical resources here

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:02.210
unlike any city in the world

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:03.610
and it doesn't start with sports stadiums

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.190
which in this day and
age most people have,

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.220
it really starts with USC and UCLA

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:08.740
which is kind of counterintuitive.

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:10.600
No city on earth has two big universities

00:28:10.600 --> 00:28:12.482
that have athletic programs at that scale

00:28:12.482 --> 00:28:15.290
that are in the city center
10 miles from each other.

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:16.842
Lots of cities have big universities,

00:28:16.842 --> 00:28:19.010
lots of cities has great universities,

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:20.400
some have athletics, some don't,

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:21.805
some are in the city center, some aren't.

00:28:21.805 --> 00:28:24.810
LA is unique and so the
combination of the scale

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:27.890
of Los Angeles, the facilities

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:29.990
we have on the sports
side but USC and UCLA

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:32.300
as a starting point means
we can host the Olympics

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:34.322
without building a new
venue and that's no,

00:28:34.322 --> 00:28:37.090
there's no sort of comma there and a but,

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:39.400
it's legitimately we don't
have to build a new venue

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:42.420
so the athletes who we will have to house

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:45.902
will move into the dorms
at UCLA for the Olympics,

00:28:45.902 --> 00:28:49.800
the media who we house will
move into the dorms at USC.

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:52.580
We've got stadiums and
practice facilities and arenas,

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:55.080
we have I mean there's
even a model Sal here

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.620
if you wanna talk about a
fun words problem you know,

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:02.350
take the Rubik's cube of of
Olympic sports and venues

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:04.790
and map it out in terms of
transportation logistics

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:06.320
and all that stuff someone
would have fun with that

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.710
but there's an environment
where we can host the Olympics

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:11.250
in Los Angeles and not use a
single venue in Orange County

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:13.920
and by the way you've got
great basketball, hockey arena,

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:17.610
great baseball stadium
not use dodger stadium

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:21.230
and not use the rose bowl and
still host the Olympics easily

00:29:21.230 --> 00:29:23.350
and so, not saying that
that's what will happen

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:24.550
but that's a real scenario

00:29:24.550 --> 00:29:27.600
and so we've got an
incredible set of facilities

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:28.711
and if we do this right,

00:29:28.711 --> 00:29:31.023
I think the economic and the cultural

00:29:31.023 --> 00:29:33.120
and the community impact
will be staggering

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:35.610
as part of our commitment to
the hosting the games of '28,

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:38.850
we agreed to fund COVID aside,

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:41.970
we agreed to fund 160
million dollars to subsidize

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:44.010
youth sports in Los Angeles
for kids in the city

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:46.545
so the youth sports
was free for everybody.

00:29:46.545 --> 00:29:49.820
That's a powerful, that's the
biggest investment in sports

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.210
in the history of this country in one city

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:55.040
and it's a powerful statement
that literally has a chance

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:56.370
to change this community forever

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:57.730
and that's just the tip of the iceberg

00:29:57.730 --> 00:30:00.768
if we do our job right in
executing the Olympics.

00:30:00.768 --> 00:30:02.720
- That's incredible.

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:05.690
I also remember the '84 Olympics very well

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:08.200
obviously watching it on TV
and I still have the memories

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.728
of they had that tall
guy to play the alien,

00:30:09.728 --> 00:30:12.230
that was a very memorable moment for me

00:30:13.124 --> 00:30:15.526
and you know what it
is, it just makes you,

00:30:15.526 --> 00:30:17.980
it captures the imagination and you know,

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:20.280
I can't imagine how cool it
is to be at the center of that

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:21.460
also how stressful it is

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:23.780
'cause the logistics you
just described don't seem,

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.270
don't seem easy but Casey--

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:28.680
- It is the big, it is
the biggest event on earth

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:30.610
I mean it's crazy the
Olympics are the biggest event

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:32.200
on earth by every measure.

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:34.490
The Paralympics are the
third biggest event on earth

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:37.460
by every measure and they
will happen in the same city

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:38.393
in 30 days.

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.210
- Wow, wow and you have got until 2028

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:44.370
so I'm hopefully we'll
be through COVID by then

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.603
and everything will be back
to normal and all of that.

00:30:47.603 --> 00:30:49.100
Well Casey thank you so much.

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:51.410
This was super valuable you
know, it gives us a lens,

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:53.890
it gives me the lens into
a world that you know

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:57.420
we enjoy but we don't understand
all of what's going on

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.500
and I could have asked you
questions for several more hours

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:02.100
but you know, thank you
and thanks for you know,

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:04.490
being a supporter of the work we do

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:07.218
and I hope there's more ways
that we can work together

00:31:07.218 --> 00:31:09.357
and figure out ways to help more folks.

00:31:09.357 --> 00:31:12.100
- Well it's my pleasure
and I appreciate you

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:15.099
and you know what a fan of
yours and all that you do,

00:31:15.099 --> 00:31:18.770
I am and proud to have
known you a long time

00:31:18.770 --> 00:31:19.858
and be a supporter

00:31:19.858 --> 00:31:23.230
and your name might be
a bad name in my house

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:24.760
'cause I tell my kids when
they ask me a question

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:25.930
I go just go on Khan Academy

00:31:25.930 --> 00:31:27.470
but other than that I love you

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.323
and always happy to support you.

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:32.373
- Wonderful.
Well thank you so much Casey.

00:31:32.373 --> 00:31:34.233
- Thank you.
- Thanks for joining.

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:38.240
Well everyone, thanks again for joining

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:40.400
what is always a fascinating conversation.

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:42.800
I learned a lot, I feel very
lucky to be able to do this

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:44.210
